## What was checked, and how

Each sampled pair is one statement of the dataset with one of its proofs, assembled into its own Lean module by the
published rule in `recipe/constants.toml`. At the pin, `{pin_lean}` with Mathlib `{pin_mathlib}` (commit
`{pin_commit}`, {pin_arch}), every pair is compiled as its own module, replayed by `leanchecker`, and audited
declaration by declaration: an axiom outside the allowlist in `recipe/constants.toml`, or a dependence on `sorryAx`, is
a failure in its own class. {aggregate} Each pair's class comes from a fixed, published decision
table in `classifier/`, never from judgement, and every record carries the raw diagnostics the table read, so anyone
can apply the table again with `PYTHONPATH=src python3 -m ledger report --policy recipe/policy/container.toml
--from-records results/records.jsonl`.

{declared}

On the project's host the build step can open no socket of any kind: sources and dependency packages are fetched in a
separate step before it, so a download is prevented rather than detected. Every fetch the pipeline makes is TCP, and a
local service that could fetch on the step's behalf (the user's D-Bus, a container daemon) is reachable only over a
Unix socket. Only the separate fetch steps reach the network, over TCP. Inside a reproduction container the same holds:
the build step can open no socket of any kind and, as on the host, writes only beneath its own directories, save that
on a kernel whose Landlock does not mediate truncation it can truncate a file outside them, which can break a run but
never reach the network; the container stands in for the host's confinement of what it reads.

